    "@id": "cm:speaking-anxiety"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "Am I declining this presentation because it is not my job, or because I am afraid of it?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/speaking-anxiety/check/2",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:speaking-anxiety"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "Have I rehearsed this out loud, standing, at least once - or only read my notes silently?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/speaking-anxiety/check/3",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:speaking-anxiety"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "Am I assuming the audience can see my nerves as clearly as I can feel them?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/speaking-anxiety/check/4",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:speaking-anxiety"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "What is the one point I need them to leave with, if everything else goes badly?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/speaking-anxiety/check/5",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:speaking-anxiety"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "Afterwards, am I judging the talk by how I felt or by what the audience actually did with it?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/check/1",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:teach-back"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "Did I ask them to say it in their own words, or did I accept a nod or a \"makes sense\"?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/check/2",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:teach-back"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "Did I frame the check as testing my explanation, not their competence?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/check/3",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:teach-back"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "Which one or two points would cause real damage if misunderstood, and did I check those?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/check/4",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:teach-back"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "When their restatement was off, did I re-explain differently rather than repeat myself louder?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/example",
   "@type": "cmo:Rewrite",
   "cmo:after": "I've thrown a lot at you, so let me check I explained it well. If the patch fails on Thursday, what are your next two moves, and who hears from you first?",
   "cmo:before": "So that's the background - escalate to Priya's team if the patch fails, and keep the client posted. Does that all make sense?",
   "cmo:situation": "A team lead hands over an urgent client escalation to a colleague before going on leave.",
   "cmo:why": "The yes/no check invites a polite \"yes\"; the open request surfaces the missing piece (who to call first) while the lead can still fix it, and frames any gap as the explainer's problem."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto",
   "@type": "schema:HowTo",
   "schema:name": "Teach-back",
   "schema:step": [
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/1"
    },
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/2"
    },
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/3"
    },
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/4"
    },
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/5"
    },
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/6"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/1",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 1,
   "schema:text": "Pick the one to three points the other person must get right to act - a deadline, a decision, a sequence of steps - rather than checking everything."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/2",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 2,
   "schema:text": "Explain each point in plain, specific language, one point at a time."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/3",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 3,
   "schema:text": "Take ownership of the check out loud, for example \"I want to make sure I explained that clearly.\""
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/4",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 4,
   "schema:text": "Ask an open request to restate or demonstrate (\"How will you walk the client through this?\"), never a yes/no question such as \"Does that make sense?\""
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/5",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 5,
   "schema:text": "Listen for gaps or errors; if you hear one, re-explain that part differently and ask again until their version is accurate."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/teach-back/howto/6",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 6,
   "schema:text": "Close by confirming the agreed version in writing where it matters (a recap message or ticket)."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/1",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:words-of-estimative-probability"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "If three readers each wrote down the percentage they think my likelihood word means, would their numbers land in the same range as mine?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/2",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:words-of-estimative-probability"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "Have I put the number next to the word, or am I relying on readers to look up a key they will probably skip?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/3",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:words-of-estimative-probability"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "Am I using \"could\", \"may\" or \"possible\" to hedge when I actually have an estimate I am reluctant to commit to?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/check/4",
   "@type": "cmo:SelfCheckQuestion",
   "cmo:questionFor": {
    "@id": "cm:words-of-estimative-probability"
   },
   "rdfs:label": "Have I kept my confidence in the evidence separate from the likelihood of the event?"
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/example",
   "@type": "cmo:Rewrite",
   "cmo:after": "We judge it unlikely (20-45%) that we hit the March 1 go-live; very likely (80-95%) by March 22. We have moderate confidence: the estimate rests on two weeks of test-defect data. Decision needed by Friday: hold the date with reduced scope, or move it.",
   "cmo:before": "There is a real possibility we could miss the March 1 go-live, although the team is working hard and it may well still be achievable.",
   "cmo:situation": "A consultant updates a client steering committee on whether a system migration will hit its go-live date.",
   "cmo:why": "The before version could be read anywhere from a small risk to a probable slip; the after version gives one shared range, separates odds from confidence, and turns the forecast into a decision the committee can make."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto",
   "@type": "schema:HowTo",
   "schema:name": "Words of estimative probability",
   "schema:step": [
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/1"
    },
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/2"
    },
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/3"
    },
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/4"
    },
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/5"
    },
    {
     "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/6"
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/1",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 1,
   "schema:text": "Agree a short likelihood scale with your team or client before you need it - for example remote (under 5%), unlikely (20-45%), roughly even (45-55%), likely (55-80%), very likely (80-95%) - with ranges that do not overlap."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/2",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 2,
   "schema:text": "Before writing a forecast, decide your own number or range first, then pick the one word on the scale whose range contains it."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/3",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 3,
   "schema:text": "Put the numeric range in brackets right after the word in the text itself (\"likely (55-80%)\"), not in a footnote, appendix or hover text."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/4",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 4,
   "schema:text": "Keep likelihood (how probable the event is) separate from confidence (how solid your evidence is), and say each in its own sentence."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/5",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 5,
   "schema:text": "Reserve \"possible\", \"could\" and \"may\" for cases where you genuinely cannot estimate the odds, and say so rather than using them as softeners."
  },
  {
   "@id": "https://github.com/john1hsu/communication-graph/id/words-of-estimative-probability/howto/6",
   "@type": "schema:HowToStep",
   "schema:position": 6,
   "schema:text": "Use the same words with the same meanings in every report, and add a short key whenever a new reader joins the distribution."
  }
 ]
}
